# What `docker build` must NOT send to the build engine.
#
# `docker build .` packs up this whole folder (the "build context") before it
# reads a single line of the Dockerfile, and `COPY . .` copies from that. Git's
# .gitignore plays no part: only this file filters the context. Without it the
# context was 65.6 MB, 60.7 MB of it target/.
#
# The build needs only: mvnw, .mvn/, pom.xml and src/.

# Build output from this machine. The image compiles its own; copying these in
# would put classes compiled on a laptop, possibly stale (the Week 8 Monday
# problem), next to or instead of the image's own.
target/

# The repository's history: every past version of every file, including any
# secret that was ever committed and later deleted. Nothing in the build reads it.
.git/

# Real settings: DB_PASSWORD and JWT_SECRET. A file copied into a build is
# stored in an image layer that anyone who pulls the image can read. The
# container gets its settings from the environment at run time instead.
.env
.env.*

# Editor, IDE and local tool settings: personal, and nothing the build reads.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
.claude/
.remember/

# Not part of the build: documentation, the Windows wrapper (the image runs
# ./mvnw), the API walk scripts (they run against a running server), and CI
# workflows.
*.md
mvnw.cmd
scripts/
.github/

# The Docker files themselves. The build never reads them from the context,
# and leaving them out means editing one doesn't invalidate the cached
# `COPY . .` layer.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
